<Base title="About & licences" description="Why Trading Tools exists, how the browser workflow fits together, and the open source + commercial licence terms for these tools.">
<section class="section-shell page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">ABOUT / BUILT AROUND A WORKFLOW</div><h1>Trading tools by SVKO.</h1><p class="lead">Trading Tools is a collection by Vladan Colovic, under the SVKO name. The collection includes CFD indicators, TradingView browser customisations, Trade Nation shortcuts and the independent Trading Statistics tool. They do not prescribe a trading strategy.</p></section>
<section class="section-shell article-page"><article class="prose"><h2>Exchange data on CFD charts</h2><p>A CFD ticker and its corresponding stock, futures or ETF symbol do not always offer the same information. Once the symbol mapping or volume formula is set up, the indicators automatically select the matching source and show its company information, volume or percentage levels alongside the CFD chart.</p><p>My main broker focus is IG and Trade Nation. Compatibility belongs to each tool and configuration; it is not a promise that every script works on every broker platform.</p><h2 id="browser">Why I use a dedicated browser</h2><p>A browser gives me the userscript customisations described here: meaningful tab names, clickable ticker text, saved AI questions and the watchlist display I prefer. I keep TradingView in a dedicated browser as part of that workflow.</p><p>You do not need a separate browser to read this site, and the tools do not depend on a claim that one browser is faster. Edge with Tampermonkey is my current workflow. Check each script's installation notes for its documented browser and manager combination.</p><p>The TradingView userscripts run on the website in a desktop browser. They do not run inside the native TradingView Desktop app. Pine indicators are a separate category.</p><h2 id="mobile">A mobile website, a desktop workflow</h2><p>Read the guides and source on your phone or tablet. Userscript installation and use require a supported desktop browser and manager. The Pine indicators are designed around my desktop workflow. TradingView mobile can render Pine indicators, but I have not reviewed or tested these indicators' mobile output.</p><h2 id="licences">Licences and attribution</h2><p>The current original website, userscripts and Pine indicator sources use the SVKO Personal Use and Commercial Licence 1.0. Open source + commercial licence means free personal use, including trading real money for your own profit, and a separate paid agreement for company and work use. Because business use is restricted, this is a source-available licence, not an OSI open-source licence.</p><ul><li><a href="/licensing/">Licence terms and commercial enquiries</a></li><li><a href="/licences/SVKO-1.0.txt">Full SVKO licence text</a></li><li><a href={`${repository}/blob/main/public/sources/LICENSING.md`}>Source licences and attribution</a></li></ul><p>Hotkeys uses knowledge of Trade From Chart's platform objects, as acknowledged in its documentation. It is a standalone userscript and does not distribute that extension. Trading Statistics loads uPlot under its own licence. Third-party licences and attribution remain applicable. Watchlist was implemented independently, inspired by another author’s watchlist idea.</p><h2>Report bugs or suggest changes</h2><p>Each tool page links directly to its source. Report a reproducible issue with the tool name, relevant version and steps. Remove private account details before sharing screenshots or records. Focused pull requests for fixes, documentation or useful changes are welcome.</p><p><a href={`${repository}/issues`}>Open an issue</a> · <a href={`${repository}/pulls`}>Browse pull requests</a></p><h2>About the illustrations</h2><p>Explanatory diagrams show symbol connections and how the tools work. These remain part of the documentation. Illustrative interface mockups use synthetic values and are labelled separately. Optional real screenshots or clips can show the actual interface alongside the diagrams. Diagrams and mockups are not live market data or account performance.</p></article></section>
</Base>
